Stage 4 – Design Complete Pseudocode (4 Marks)
Algorithm: MaximumEnergySaving

Objective: Select a set of mutually compatible jobs to maximise the total energy-saving value without selecting overlapping or incompatible jobs.

The algorithm uses Dynamic Programming to calculate the maximum achievable energy savings and reconstruct the selected jobs.

Pseudocode

text
ALGORITHM MaximumEnergySaving

BEGIN

    READ N

    FOR i ← 1 TO N DO
        READ JobID[i]
        READ StartTime[i]
        READ FinishTime[i]
        READ EnergySaving[i]
    END FOR

    SORT jobs in ascending order of FinishTime

    FOR i ← 1 TO N DO

        Compatible[i] ← 0

        FOR j ← i-1 DOWNTO 1 DO

            IF FinishTime[j] <= StartTime[i] THEN

                Compatible[i] ← j

                EXIT FOR

            END IF

        END FOR

    END FOR

    DP[0] ← 0

    FOR i ← 1 TO N DO

        IncludeValue ← EnergySaving[i] +
                       DP[Compatible[i]]

        ExcludeValue ← DP[i-1]

        DP[i] ← MAX(IncludeValue, ExcludeValue)

    END FOR

    MaximumSaving ← DP[N]

    SelectedCount ← 0

    i ← N

    WHILE i > 0 DO

        IncludeValue ← EnergySaving[i] +
                       DP[Compatible[i]]

        ExcludeValue ← DP[i-1]

        IF IncludeValue > ExcludeValue THEN

            SelectedCount ← SelectedCount + 1

            SelectedJobs[SelectedCount] ← JobID[i]

            i ← Compatible[i]

        ELSE

            i ← i - 1

        END IF

    END WHILE

    WRITE "Selected Jobs"

    FOR i ← SelectedCount DOWNTO 1 DO

        WRITE SelectedJobs[i]

    END FOR

    WRITE "Maximum Total Energy Saving:",
          MaximumSaving

    RETURN SelectedJobs, MaximumSaving

END ALGORITHM
Explanation of the Algorithm
Input: Read the number of jobs, job IDs, start times, finish times and energy-saving values.

Preprocessing: Sort all jobs in ascending order of finishing time.

Compatibility calculation: For each job, identify the latest earlier job that finishes before or exactly when the current job starts.

Initialisation: Set DP[0] ← 0, since no jobs produce zero energy savings.

Decision logic: Calculate two possibilities:

Include the current job and add its energy savings to the best compatible previous solution.

Exclude the current job and retain the previous optimal solution.

State update: Store the larger value in DP[i].

Reconstruction: Trace backwards through the DP states to identify the selected jobs.

Output: Display the selected job IDs and maximum total energy-saving value.

Important Conditions
Compatible jobs: A previous job is compatible when FinishTime[j] ≤ StartTime[i].

Incompatible jobs: Jobs that overlap cannot both be selected.

Termination: The DP calculation terminates after all jobs are processed. Reconstruction terminates when i = 0.

Maximum value: DP[N] stores the maximum total energy-saving value.

Note: This algorithm assumes each job has a start time, finish time and non-negative energy-saving value. If two jobs have equal include and exclude values, the algorithm chooses the exclusion option; either choice preserves the maximum value.


